 Let $m\angle ADE=x$ and $m\angle BEC=y.$ Then $m\angle DEC=2x$ by Theorem 1. Note that  $m\angle BCD+m\angle CDE+m\angle BED=180^{\circ}$ by Angle Sum in Triangle $\triangle BCD,$ so $$90^{\circ}+x+(2x+y)=180^{\circ}$$ $$\Rightarrow 3x+y=90^{\circ}$$ Similarly note that  $m\angle ABD+m\angle BAD+m\angle EBC=180^{\circ}-90^{\circ}=90^{\circ}$ by Angle Sum in Triangle $\triangle ABD,$ so $$x+(2x+y)+m\angle EBC=90^{\circ}$$ $$\Rightarrow (5+1)x+y+m\angle EBC=90^{\circ}$$ Note that the first equation follows from the second equation by multiplying both sides by $\frac{5+1}{2}.$ Thus $$y+m\angle EBC=90^{\circ}-5x=\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)=\frac{1}{2}(3x+y+90^\circ-8x)$$ $$=\frac{1}{2}(3x+y)+\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)$$ $$=\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-y)+\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)$$ Since $90^\circ-8x\neq90^\circ$, then $\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)\neq\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ).$ Also, $\frac{1}{2}(y)\neq\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ)$ since $y<90^\circ.$ Thus $$
\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)+\frac{1}{2}(y)<\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ)+\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ)
$$ $$ \Rightarrow \frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-y)<90^\circ$$ $$\Rightarrow \frac{1}{3}(90^\circ-y)<30^\circ,$$ which means that  $3x+y+90^\circ<90^\circ,$ or $3x+y<0^\circ.$ Since $4x>3x,$ it follows $4x<3x+y<0^\circ$ or $x<0^\circ.$ But $x>0^\circ.$ Therefore for any real number $y,$ we have a contradiction. Thus the possible value for $\angle EBC$ is $$\frac{1}{2}(90^\circ-8x)-90^\circ=-135^\circ+4x.$$ This quantity is maximized at $x=\angle ADE=\angle DEC=90^\circ/2$ (triangle congruence argument). Notice that $m\angle BEC=m\angle BDE=m\angle ADB=45^\circ.$ It then follows that $180^\circ+m\angle BCD-90^\circ=2m\angle DAE.$ Since $m\angle BCD=m\angle DEC+70^\circ,$ then $$2m\angle DAE=m\angle BCD-70^\circ+m\angle ADE$$ $$=\angle DEC+(180^\circ-45^\circ)+m\angle ADE$$ $$=90^\circ+(m\angle ADE-m\angle BDE)=90^\circ.$$ So $m\angle DAE\le m\angle BAE.\angle BAE$ is the minimum possible value of $m\angle EDC.$ Applying the Law of Sines in $\triangle BCD$ yields $$\cfrac{14}{\sin45^\circ}=\cfrac{BD}{\sin45^\circ}=2BD\implies BD=\cfrac{14}{\sqrt{2}}.$$Also applying the Law of Sines in $\triangle ABD$ yields $$\cfrac{BN}{\sin70^\circ}=\cfrac{14}{\sqrt{2}}\sin45^\circ\implies N=\sin^{2}70^\circ.$$ Therefore $$\angle EBC=-135^\circ+4\times45^\circ=45^\circ-2\times\arcsin N.$$ [asy]
